// ALG1 Unit 06 Lesson 05 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Scatterplots and Trend Analysis
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.DF.5; MLA.A1.DF.7

::ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.5]
A scatterplot rises from left to right. What type of association does it show?
{
~A. Negative association#Negative association falls as x increases.
=B. Positive association#Correct. Rising from left to right means y tends to increase as x increases.
~C. No association#No association has no clear pattern.
~D. Causation#Association alone does not prove causation.
}

::ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.7]
A line of fit is y = 4x + 10. What is the predicted y-value when x = 6?
{
~A. 14#This adds 4 and 10 but does not multiply by x.
~B. 20#This uses x incorrectly in the expression.
~C. 24#This calculates 4(6) but forgets to add 10.
=D. 34#Correct. Substitute x = 6: y = 4(6) + 10 = 34.
}

::ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.5]
Which statement is safest when two variables are correlated?
{
=A. The variables are associated, but one may not cause the other#Correct. Correlation alone does not prove causation.
~B. The first variable always causes the second#A correlation does not prove a cause.
~C. The second variable always causes the first#The direction of causation is not proven by correlation alone.
~D. There is no relationship at all#Correlation means there is some association.
}

::ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.7]
In y = 3x + 12, x is hours practiced and y is score. What does the slope mean?
{
~A. The starting score is 3#The starting value is the y-intercept, 12.
~B. The score decreases by 3 per hour#A positive slope means increase.
=C. The predicted score increases by 3 points per hour#Correct. The slope gives the change in y for each 1-unit increase in x.
~D. The score is always 12#The score changes with x.
}

::ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L05_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.5]
A scatterplot has points spread randomly with no upward or downward pattern. What association is shown?
{
~A. Strong positive association#A strong positive pattern rises clearly.
=B. No clear association#Correct. A random cloud has no clear trend.
~C. Strong negative association#A strong negative pattern falls clearly.
~D. Perfect linear association#A perfect linear pattern would lie on a line.
}
